# .ai-core/config.env
# Project-specific settings for setup-ai-core. Not tracked by git.

# WHERE THE PROJECT'S ISSUES AND BOARD LIVE: "github" (the default), whose tracker.sh the issue and board
# commands call, or "forgejo", whose issue commands run in Node from lib/forgejo/tracker and which has no
# board. "forgejo" takes the instance and the token command that FORGE below names.
TRACKER="github"

# WHERE THE PROJECT'S CODE LIVES: the folder of lib/ whose forge.sh answers the repository, CI and
# sign-in calls: "github" (the default) or "forgejo". With "forgejo", name the instance (scheme and host,
# no path) and the command that prints an access token; no value of them has a default. git signs in
# through ai-core git-credential, which runs that command each time git asks, so the token is in no file.
# A pipeline that posts the commit status `check` on the commit is what `ai-core release` waits for.
FORGE="github"
# FORGEJO_URL="https://forge.example.test"
# FORGEJO_TOKEN_COMMAND="cat $HOME/.config/forgejo/token"

# THE AGENTS THIS PROJECT SERVES, separated by spaces. init deploys the pointer file of each one
# named here and no other.
# Known names: claude, codex, antigravity, openhands, gemini, cursor, windsurf, copilot.
# Empty: every pointer file.
AGENTS="claude codex antigravity openhands"

# Whether session-start asks the origins for releases and new commits (ai-core update --check):
# "session-start" (default) or "never". Nothing is updated by that; the answer is a line.
UPDATE_CHECK="session-start"

# The map (ai-core map): the agent CLI that writes it, claude, codex or agy, and its model when
# not the CLI's default. AI_CORE_MAP_TOOL in the environment names the CLI of one machine instead.
MAP_TOOL="claude"
MAP_MODEL=""

# THE USAGE LIMIT, in percent of any of the account's usage windows. At it every session using that
# limited account or model finishes the step in hand, commits, reports and waits for the reset.
# `ai-core usage` exits 3 there. The hour a window reaches the limit at the measured burn is shown
# and stops no start. `--tool claude`, `--tool codex` or `--tool agy` selects whose windows it reads.
# Every tool's windows and the limit are shown as percent used. 92 when unset.
USAGE_STOP_AT="92"

# THE ECOSYSTEMS IN THE ORDER THE OWNER STARTS THEIR COORDINATORS, by the tool names of
# .ai-core/models.tsv. A package belongs to the ecosystem whose coordinator planned it and stays
# there; no coordinator hands a package on. "claude codex agy" when unset.
ECOSYSTEM_ORDER="claude codex agy"

# THE LOAD A CHECK MAY START AT, read by the push gate before it starts the repository's
# scripts/check.sh: while the 1-minute load average is at or above PRE_PUSH_LOAD_LIMIT the gate waits,
# at most PRE_PUSH_LOAD_WAIT_SECONDS, and then starts the check and says it started above the limit.
# Both are read again at every poll. A sample reserves no CPU: the load can rise after it.
# PRE_PUSH_LOAD_LIMIT: 24 when unset, an absolute load, not a share of the cores; 0 turns the wait off.
# PRE_PUSH_LOAD_WAIT_SECONDS: 300 when unset; 0 starts the check at once.
# With either at 0 the load is still measured once for the record of the run; a reading that fails
# is said and recorded as -, and does not refuse the push. With both above 0, a load that cannot be
# read refuses the push.
PRE_PUSH_LOAD_LIMIT="24"
PRE_PUSH_LOAD_WAIT_SECONDS="300"

# THE ROUNDS OF A LANDING: when the default branch moved while the gate of a round ran, `ai-core integrate-issue`
# fetches it again, merges onto its new tip and runs the whole gate on that merge, at most INTEGRATE_MAX_ROUNDS
# times, the first attempt counted: 3 when unset; a whole number from 1 to 999999999; 1 is a single attempt that
# stops with nothing pushed and asks to run again. It is read again before every round, from this file only.
INTEGRATE_MAX_ROUNDS="3"

# THE PUBLISHED COPY OF A NEIGHBOUR, which a check reads through .., is brought to its origin's newest commit
# before the check starts and stays there while the check runs. A gate or init that must move a copy keeps the gates
# that arrive later out and waits for the checks that read it when it asked, at least
# NEIGHBOUR_LOCK_WAIT_SECONDS: 600 when unset; a whole number from 0 to 999999999. A copy that needs no move waits for
# nobody. A check that hangs is waited for only that long, and then refused or named; a fetch of the neighbour that
# another gate or init runs is waited for NEIGHBOUR_LOCK_WAIT_SECONDS at most, a fetch of its own as long as git takes.
# The wait is also at least one and a half times the longest check the run log of the push gate
# records for the last 14 days, so one outlier ages out by itself, counted as no less than 1320 seconds, so 600
# waits 1980 seconds without a longer check on record. 0
# tries once and does not wait.
NEIGHBOUR_LOCK_WAIT_SECONDS="600"

# THE FILES WHOSE CHANGE REACHES THE DEFAULT BRANCH BY A PLAIN PUSH, read by the push gate from this file only, at every
# push: paths or shell patterns separated by white space, relative to the repository root, matched against the whole path,
# case counted. * stands for any characters, a slash included, ? for one, [...] for one of a set; without them a pattern
# names one file. The gate admits a plain push to the default branch, without a landing mark, where every new commit,
# read on its own, touches only files that match; a commit that touches any other file, or this file itself, makes the
# push a plain one, which is refused. The commit still names its issue or carries its No-issue reason, and the
# repository's own checks still run. A path that is absolute, or has an empty, . or .. component, is refused by name.
# Empty: none.
DIRECT_PUSH_FILES=""

# THE START CHECK, run by the person in charge on the bundle of a package's branches before it
# lands: the product starts on everything that ships together, so a strict side never reaches a
# release before its additive side. A command of the project, run from the project folder with the
# worktree paths as its arguments; exit 0 is the pass. Empty: the project names none, and the land
# record says so.
START_CHECK=""

# WHERE THE WORK IS LIVE, read by `ai-core status` for every card in testing:
# each environment with the pattern of its release tags, the last stage first, e.g.
# "prod=deploy/prod/* test=deploy/test/*". A card's work is live on an environment from the first of
# its tags that contains the newest commit on the default branch naming the issue. A repository
# with no release tag of its own, live where it lands, names `landed` instead ("prod=landed"): its
# landing on the default branch is its release, for status and for status-sync. A repository that
# is delivered by something else, the release of another repository that pins it or a redeploy
# that fetches it, names `command` ("prod=command test=command"): its delivery is what the
# executable of LIVE_RELEASE_COMMAND answers. Environment names are spelled as written, `prod` and
# `Prod` are two. A card is read by the list of the clone of its repository where that names an
# environment, and otherwise by the project folder's list, the LIVE_TAGS of the project folder's
# own .ai-core/config.env, whichever checkout runs the command; where neither names one, status
# shows the card as landed and status-sync reads every tag. status walks every environment of the
# list, status-sync takes the first one. status reads a card whose landing clone has an environment
# set to `command` by that clone's whole list instead, from the landing record of its issue, and a
# `command` in the project folder's list alone runs no reader of a clone that did not declare it.
LIVE_TAGS=""
# THE READER OF THE `command` ENVIRONMENTS OF THIS CLONE'S LIVE_TAGS, from this file and never from
# the environment: one executable file, an absolute path or one relative to the clone, no default.
# It is started in the clone, not through a shell, with the full OWNER/REPO and the environment as
# two arguments, and it only reads. It prints one JSON object of nonempty strings:
#   repository   as it was given          environment  as it was given
#   commit       the full object ID, in that repository, of the newest completed delivery
#   releasedAt   when that delivery completed, UTC YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ, not in the future
#   source       the release or the successful run it inspected
# or JSON null with exit 0 where there is no completed delivery. For a pin it is the commit the
# newest completed release of the carrying repository pins, read from that release's own contents;
# for a redeploy it is the commit the last successful run fetched, a rollback included, never an
# attempt that failed or is pending and never the tip of the default branch. Anything else is an
# error: the card is shown as unknown (status) or stays (status-sync), and nothing stands in for
# it. The harness checks the shape and the identity; whether the reader told the truth about a
# deployment is the project's, and so is the deadline of each request it makes.
# status-sync closes the card when its landing is in the delivered commit and a trusted
# "Proven on <environment>:" comment of the first environment, whatever the case, came later than
# the landing record and releasedAt. status reads the landing of every card in testing from the
# tracker, one request set per card, in a project folder where a clone declares `command`.
LIVE_RELEASE_COMMAND=""
# The hours a later environment of LIVE_TAGS may carry a card the first one does not, before status
# marks its proof there overdue, read from the project folder's own .ai-core/config.env. 24 when unset.
PROOF_HOURS="24"
